% an euclidean division. Since the rest $\epsilon_{1k}$ of the division
% $w_{1k}=q[w_{1k}/q]+\epsilon_{1k}$ satisfies
% $\epsilon_{1k}<\epsilon_2$, it follows that the greatest divisor $q$ 
% of $n$ is a solution to problem $P_k$. 

Reciprocally, a solution $q$ of $P_k$
  is a divisor of $n$ different from $1$ since $w_1 \mod q=r_1$. 
  This divisor of $n$ is not
  $n$ since the condition $\epsilon_{sk}\in [a,b]$ is not satisfied for
  $q=n$. 
  Thus a polynomial time algorithm that solves $Problem\
  4$ returns a strict divisor $q$ of $n$ when applied to
  $P_k$. Hence the
  factorisation of $n$ in polynomial time. 

A priori, we don't know which problem $P_k$ satisfies $(*)$ in the
list $P_1,\dots,P_r$. We thus run a multi-threaded algorithm which tries to
  solve in parallel the problems $P_1,\dots,P_r$
and which stops as soon as it finds a solution for one problem. 
\end{dem}



